WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
Okay guys, in this problem, an engineer is designing a building that requires a simply

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.359
supported beam to be used in part of the roof design.

00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:15.120
This beam will receive a uniformly distributed load across its length.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.160
The client informs the engineer that the original building plans have been modified, and

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.560
therefore the beam will need to be 50% longer than the original length.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.760
All other characteristics of the beam will remain the same.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.920
How much greater will the maximum bending moment be in the new beam than in the original

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.079
design?

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:43.800
So the very first thing we can do here is go to our civil engineering reference handbook.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:52.000
For my version, it's page 34, which brings us to simply supported beam slopes and deflections.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.920
So the maximum moment there for the type of beam that is described.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.520
So let's draw out the beam real fast.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.320
I would just take a screenshot for you, but I don't want the NCES to ever come in here and say

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.240
that I'm sharing parts of their book without their permission.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:15.520
So instead, you guys have to deal with a really badly drawn beam, and I'm sorry about that.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.520
Anyway, so this is what type of beam is being described, and we can see from the image

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.719
that the maximum deflection is going to be in the middle.

00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:38.200
And the equation in the PDF as well is even written out.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.000
So we have the moment, sorry, that's a bad M moment.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:53.719
The maximum here at the center is going to be equal to W times L squared over eight.

00:01:53.719 --> 00:02:02.519
Now all we have to do is look at, so this would be the length, and if length L is the

00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:06.480
length of the original beam here.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.759
And then we want to look at a beam that's 50% longer.

00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:21.719
We can create a new max moment and say W times 1.5 L squared over eight.

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:28.520
And if we solve that and expand the equation just a little bit, we get 2.25 L squared

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.400
also over eight.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:37.759
And we can see by comparing these two equations that all of these terms would cancel out,

00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:41.159
except for the 2.25.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:48.079
So the maximum bending moment in beam number two would be 2.25 times greater than the maximum

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:50.960
bending moment in beam number one.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.920
And that is all you need to do to solve this problem.
